From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Alan Bradburne <abradburne@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 12:52:02 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 3/4] mmc: rockchip_dw_mmc: UHS SDR104 for the RK3568 SD card

U-Boot read the RG DS+ card at High Speed, 50 MHz at 3.3 V: 0.33 s for a
7.57 MB kernel. Linux already runs the same card at UHS SDR104 (150 MHz,
1.8 V). Teach U-Boot to do the same; the kernel then loads in 107 ms.

- The Rockchip dw_mmc glue offers SDR104 (and SDR25/SDR12) when the DT
  says sd-uhs-sdr104, vqmmc feeds a Rockchip IO domain and the CRU phase
  registers are known (RK3568 only here). Anything missing leaves High
  Speed. SDR50 isn't offered: 100 MHz needs a 200 MHz CRU clock, which
  the SD clock mux can't make.
- The IO domain follows vqmmc in the safe order, as Linux's io-domain
  notifiers do: 3.3 V pads before vqmmc rises, 1.8 V pads only after it
  falls. rockchip-io-domain.c gains rockchip_iodomain_set_voltage() for
  it, probing the domain on first use (SPL doesn't probe probe-after-bind
  devices).
- Drive (90 degrees, 180 at SDR104) and sample phases are written to the
  CRU's SDMMC0_CON0/1, as Linux's clk-mmc-phase.c does; U-Boot's clock
  drivers have no phase support. SDR104 tunes the sample phase over 36
  steps of 10 degrees and takes the middle of the longest good run.
- dw_mmc core: the clock is really gated during the voltage switch
  (set_ios ignored clk_disable), no automatic clock stop while
  switching, the UHS_REG 1.8 V bit, the card read threshold at SDR104,
  and a data error no longer waits 1 s for the IDMAC (tuning fails many
  reads on purpose).
- A card switched to 1.8 V only goes back to 3.3 V when its power is
  cut, which the boot command's High Speed retry (mmc dev 1 0 0) needs:
  the glue cuts the always-on vcc3v3_sd, past the regulator's always-on
  check and enable count, only in that case. Tested with a forced
  retry: power off 28 ms, card back at legacy 25 MHz, kernel boots.

Upstream status: candidate, as a series to split (io-domain API, dw_mmc
core fixes, Rockchip phases and tuning, the power-cycle retry).
